etween fluxes and gradients is encoded in SymPy. At every stage the mathematics is visible, inspectable, and differentiable. The framework handles Jacobians, C code generation, and PETSc integration. You handle the physics.","key":"TwoDvGCPXY"}],"key":"mIJvymIUT4"}],"key":"o4GPoItKQ6"}],"key":"oveu4BTLp0"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/craton-formation-and-the-onset-of-plate-tectonics/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Craton Formation and the Onset of Plate Tectonics","key":"oKOwgdxqWz"}],"urlSource":"/craton-formation-and-the-onset-of-plate-tectonics/","key":"ehQoY50ULE"}],"identifier":"craton-formation-and-the-onset-of-plate-tectonics","label":"Craton Formation and the Onset of Plate Tectonics","html_id":"craton-formation-and-the-onset-of-plate-tectonics","implicit":true,"key":"thIra6Akwi"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[],"key":"BY5l8QOoxI"}],"key":"OBXK0KJHbz"}],"key":"xuMnYTkDpE"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/finding-particles-in-a-distributed-unstructured-mesh/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Finding Particles in a Distributed, Unstructured Mesh","key":"GHVSeEmcHg"}],"urlSource":"/finding-particles-in-a-distributed-unstructured-mesh/","key":"MnymLbLaXM"}],"identifier":"finding-particles-in-a-distributed-unstructured-mesh","label":"Finding Particles in a Distributed, Unstructured Mesh","html_id":"finding-particles-in-a-distributed-unstructured-mesh","implicit":true,"key":"naphftd1Ip"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"It can be difficult to figure out where you are in an unstructured mesh of triangles or tetrahedra; worse when the mesh is distributed in parallel. We have to solve this problem for particle-in-cell type codes such as Underworld3. This is how we do it.","key":"aDpN3vHTh9"}],"key":"LfsiZkDoCJ"}],"key":"v4vwPyjg9F"}],"key":"Pi3QHMBmrj"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/free-surface-in-underworld/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Free surface in Underworld","key":"docgSGLJsD"}],"urlSource":"/free-surface-in-underworld/","key":"U1AJEZP5cK"}],"identifier":"free-surface-in-underworld","label":"Free surface in Underworld","html_id":"free-surface-in-underworld","implicit":true,"key":"ery8zVwwqR"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[],"key":"MHQuZlrD91"}],"key":"hC0UrDnCKO"}],"key":"YBlAh9uAbK"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/getting-started-60-seconds-to-underworld/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Getting started: 60 seconds to Underworld","key":"nBq0938j8J"}],"urlSource":"/getting-started-60-seconds-to-underworld/","key":"QlmSXE0Fax"}],"identifier":"getting-started-60-seconds-to-underworld","label":"Getting started: 60 seconds to Underworld","html_id":"getting-started-60-seconds-to-underworld","implicit":true,"key":"WlTbLzmqso"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"As the old truism states, the best way to learn is by doing. This guide can get you from zero to hero in 60 seconds - give or take.","key":"TPpGIpGiAr"}],"key":"dM81VgqXjG"}],"key":"kxtJedB9i6"}],"key":"JcOXgxFqQu"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/getting-started-with-pull-requests/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Getting started with Pull requests","key":"GteC4F1Pl6"}],"urlSource":"/getting-started-with-pull-requests/","key":"Yuk1jrvhqi"}],"identifier":"getting-started-with-pull-requests","label":"Getting started with Pull requests","html_id":"getting-started-with-pull-requests","implicit":true,"key":"tQugK2h7nr"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[],"key":"FYBxdswMKI"}],"key":"y1X6dxMjRn"}],"key":"Rr16jwUKMQ"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/how-many-processors-should-we-use-to-solve-problem-x/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How many processors should we use to solve Problem X?","key":"seNJCZI8He"}],"urlSource":"/how-many-processors-should-we-use-to-solve-problem-x/","key":"xjTdpnDRHU"}],"identifier":"how-many-processors-should-we-use-to-solve-problem-x","label":"How many processors should we use to solve Problem X?","html_id":"how-many-processors-should-we-use-to-solve-problem-x","implicit":true,"key":"Y7ik0TUenP"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Parallel computation puts many CPUs to work on solving a problem much more quickly than one CPU alone. But this only works if the tasks are carefully scheduled and the additional CPUs are not waiting around for something to do. How do we choose the right number of processors for a given problem ?","key":"c5AJMDKtNS"}],"key":"oTmKIOTxOw"}],"key":"jMcsAqkl09"}],"key":"npG7TtRphj"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/how-to-install-underworld-on-mac-osx-big-sur-apple-silicon-m1/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How to install Underworld on Mac OSX (Apple Silicon M1)","key":"xATUOcpVye"}],"urlSource":"/how-to-install-underworld-on-mac-osx-big-sur-apple-silicon-m1/","key":"tR6YlpsB07"}],"identifier":"how-to-install-underworld-on-mac-osx-apple-silicon-m1","label":"How to install Underworld on Mac OSX (Apple Silicon M1)","html_id":"how-to-install-underworld-on-mac-osx-apple-silicon-m1","implicit":true,"key":"LFBIp58Zj9"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"The new generation of Apple Mac comes with the new Apple Silicon (M1) chip which has an Arm architecture (as opposed to the older generation that had i386 Intel processor). This brings all manner of troubles and requirements for the development of codes.","key":"ybH7DUAZ2S"}],"key":"Zv5F9woOtk"}],"key":"l88NVj9Xvu"}],"key":"JUFTBrDgEo"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/how-underworld3-turns-sympy-into-c/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"How Underworld3 Turns SymPy into C","key":"plemTF44za"}],"urlSource":"/how-underworld3-turns-sympy-into-c/","key":"n6qNzCzaby"}],"identifier":"how-underworld3-turns-sympy-into-c","label":"How Underworld3 Turns SymPy into C","html_id":"how-underworld3-turns-sympy-into-c","implicit":true,"key":"ZlsEyK6jLZ"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"What actually happens between the moment you write a mathematical expression in underworld3's python layer and the moment PETSc receives a finite element term in the form of compiled C code ?","key":"HyxLKmG11I"}],"key":"AvDvnftkjQ"}],"key":"Pgnue8yAhc"}],"key":"pUcf5GOUzp"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/ismip-hom-benchmark-experiments-using-underworld/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"ISMIP-HOM benchmark experiments using Underworld","key":"wx2Ahtlr4Y"}],"urlSource":"/ismip-hom-benchmark-experiments-using-underworld/","key":"IBw3n1eHFp"}],"identifier":"ismip-hom-benchmark-experiments-using-underworld","label":"ISMIP-HOM benchmark experiments using Underworld","html_id":"ismip-hom-benchmark-experiments-using-underworld","implicit":true,"key":"rDIHAk8F7o"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[],"key":"cbAN3oOkx3"}],"key":"OUcfN3y20E"}],"key":"nb9OkZUZLC"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/mesh-variables-and-petsc-vectors-keeping-arrays-in-sync/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Mesh Variables and PETSc Vectors: Keeping Arrays in Sync","key":"AlUoFPbQo8"}],"urlSource":"/mesh-variables-and-petsc-vectors-keeping-arrays-in-sync/","key":"MrTZ9Q1pj1"}],"identifier":"mesh-variables-and-petsc-vectors-keeping-arrays-in-sync","label":"Mesh Variables and PETSc Vectors: Keeping Arrays in Sync","html_id":"mesh-variables-and-petsc-vectors-keeping-arrays-in-sync","implicit":true,"key":"DFXL1TL5BT"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One of the less glamorous but important problems in a finite element framework is this: how does the user assign values to a field variable, and how does the framework ensure that PETSc sees those values correctly, in parallel, without the user needing to keep track themselves?","key":"D0PsEFiKcg"}],"key":"SNOPS0Aymv"}],"key":"rcc91rYcUY"}],"key":"hvmPaKBiZm"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/moving-the-mesh-without-remaking-it/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Moving the Mesh Without Remaking It","key":"pjBJRysvNU"}],"urlSource":"/moving-the-mesh-without-remaking-it/","key":"gh1ShCHUW3"}],"identifier":"moving-the-mesh-without-remaking-it","label":"Moving the Mesh Without Remaking It","html_id":"moving-the-mesh-without-remaking-it","implicit":true,"key":"VdyvREKfdy"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"There are three ways to put resolution where a calculation needs it: rebuild the mesh, subdivide it, or move its nodes. The third conserves the node budget and leaves the connectivity, the parallel partition and the multigrid hierarchy untouched — nothing is created or destroyed, only relocated. How that redistribution works in Underworld3, what it is worth in numbers, and where its ceiling is.","key":"X5HufffAuL"}],"key":"odE8wS8PeJ"}],"key":"A5oagTXbN6"}],"key":"dL5zlZMQva"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/new-features-of-the-surface-coupling-framework-in-underworld-2/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"New features of the surface-coupling framework in Underworld 2","key":"fb5waEHVLh"}],"urlSource":"/new-features-of-the-surface-coupling-framework-in-underworld-2/","key":"faJqHCO1O2"}],"identifier":"new-features-of-the-surface-coupling-framework-in-underworld-2","label":"New features of the surface-coupling framework in Underworld 2","html_id":"new-features-of-the-surface-coupling-framework-in-underworld-2","implicit":true,"key":"G97nf19qd9"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[],"key":"VExj9nmfTt"}],"key":"URSGpfsDjB"}],"key":"FYNK6yrQdo"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/our-journey-from-underworld2-to-underworld3/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Our Journey from Underworld2 to Underworld3","key":"QnwqnCa6fe"}],"urlSource":"/our-journey-from-underworld2-to-underworld3/","key":"EMZM8l1m2I"}],"identifier":"our-journey-from-underworld2-to-underworld3","label":"Our Journey from Underworld2 to Underworld3","html_id":"our-journey-from-underworld2-to-underworld3","implicit":true,"key":"PqFKttlu8Z"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Underworld is a code for geodynamics — mantle convection, lithospheric deformation, subduction, ice flow. We solve coupled, nonlinear PDEs with complex rheologies using Lagrangian particles to track material history. The project has been running for 20 years: why did we start again ?","key":"kKulpu8FWD"}],"key":"RXfBiJTSpj"}],"key":"qLf6xsrvz4"}],"key":"r3xvFm7bcV"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/particles-in-underworld3/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Particles in Underworld3","key":"E1zCiZ99A1"}],"urlSource":"/particles-in-underworld3/","key":"DNTQAPOYNc"}],"identifier":"particles-in-underworld3","label":"Particles in Underworld3","html_id":"particles-in-underworld3","implicit":true,"key":"uIGEexq47T"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Underworld is built around the idea of active Lagrangian tracer particles that carry history and composition information as the material deforms. How do we combine this information with our symbolic mathematical framework ?","key":"CsgnvECAGI"}],"key":"FNpPoeZoVa"}],"key":"I3tJORKqAj"}],"key":"hkVQEYhbO4"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/physical-units-in-computational-geodynamics/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Physical Units in Computational Geodynamics","key":"rEyIkuXzOf"}],"urlSource":"/physical-units-in-computational-geodynamics/","key":"wL0HAtWFUA"}],"identifier":"physical-units-in-computational-geodynamics","label":"Physical Units in Computational Geodynamics","html_id":"physical-units-in-computational-geodynamics","implicit":true,"key":"rlgpg8H1iX"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Non-dimensionalisation (rewriting problems to make them re-scalable from lab to the real world) is a specialised task that all modellers confront at some point. It is important for accurate and efficient numerical solutions. Can we take away the pain that comes with the task ?","key":"dYcAxXRjSP"}],"key":"dl3WN6rbb6"}],"key":"NhGEs2vIBH"}],"key":"HhirPW6yRS"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/running-underworld-in-a-browser/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Running Underworld in a Browser: Any Repository, Any Version","key":"Gd2F2ZJpWY"}],"urlSource":"/running-underworld-in-a-browser/","key":"ZgSukYBSde"}],"identifier":"running-underworld-in-a-browser-any-repository-any-version","label":"Running Underworld in a Browser: Any Repository, Any Version","html_id":"running-underworld-in-a-browser-any-repository-any-version","implicit":true,"key":"ZmUMJdiuTc"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"One link opens Underworld in a browser, with any public repository pulled in beside it and any released version underneath. Nothing is installed, and nothing has to be added to the repository being launched. We use it for teaching, for workshops, and for anyone who would rather run the model from a paper than read about it.","key":"L9BBMBh2je"}],"key":"kwNLDQ62v7"}],"key":"f4Fnk4ovQv"}],"key":"BSkvCvGAh1"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry","children":[{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-text","children":[{"type":"heading","depth":2,"children":[{"type":"link","url":"/scaling-in-underworld/","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Scaling in Underworld","key":"aKDNZHM2kQ"}],"urlSource":"/scaling-in-underworld/","key":"c1NkGC3SSv"}],"identifier":"scaling-in-underworld","label":"Scaling in Underworld","html_id":"scaling-in-underworld","implicit":true,"key":"nt76GT4XU5"},{"type":"div","class":"uwtn-entry-summary","children":[{"type":"text","value":"To test scalability we run weak scaling tests on various HPC machines to check the numerical framework remains robust when pushing for higher fidelity models.","key":"PmZunFQZwJ"}],"key":"gjQ99cb4TW"}],"key"